<description>
Extend each colored marker pixel into a rightward alternating marker/grey stripe ending at the grid edge.

Input structure
- A dominant background with single-cell non-background marker pixels.
- Each marker row contains exactly one marker pixel.
- The marker pixels are not grey; grey is reserved for the alternating separator cells in the output.

Rule
- Preserve all cells to the left of each marker.
- From each marker through the right edge of its row, write the marker color on even offsets from the marker and grey on odd offsets.
- Rows with no marker remain unchanged.

Key insight
- The marker is the start of a horizontal ray; distance parity from the marker determines whether a cell receives the marker color or grey.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single-cell non-background marker pixels, one per active row",
  "path": "horizontal ray from each marker to the right edge of its row",
  "trajectory": "paint the ray continuously, alternating marker color on even offsets from the marker and grey on odd offsets",
  "transform": "keep rows without markers and cells left of each marker unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 7-10 rows x 8-12 cols
  background: BLACK (0)
  markers:
    count: 1-4 single-cell non-background pixels
    colors: distinct per marker, any non-BLACK non-GREY color
    placement: one marker per row; column >= 1 and <= ncols-4 (ray length >= 4) [observed]
    rows: not row 0 or last row [observed]; adjacent marker rows allowed
  separator_color: GREY (5), reserved for output only; never appears in input
  transformation: |
    For each marker at (r, c0) with color C, fill cells from c0 to right edge:
      offset 0,2,4,... -> C; offset 1,3,5,... -> GREY
    Rows without markers and cells left of each marker are unchanged.

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (input structure is simple enough)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid dimensions in range [7..10] x [8..12].
    2. Fill grid with BLACK.
    3. Pick marker count k in [1..4].
    4. Choose k distinct rows from [1..nrows-2].
    5. For each chosen row, pick a column in [1..ncols-4] and a unique non-BLACK non-GREY color.
    6. Place each marker as a single pixel.
```
</generation_spec>
